      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right to be cautious; distinguishing between automated routines and external remote commands can be challenging. If you have previously exported logs, this can be a starting point in comparing whether the commands appeared while you or your ex was at home. Check the timestamps against the known presence times to look for overlaps.\n\nRegarding identifying the origin of commands, most smart home systems allow you to check the IP addresses or device nicknames that sent a command. If you're using a Wi-Fi network, capturing and examining your network traffic with a network monitoring tool is the route to pursue. This equates to seeing who's sending commands over your network. However, analyzing network traffic safely and effectively typically requires specialized knowledge to avoid breaching privacy even unintentionally.\n\nA more straightforward approach is to review the history on your hub or through your routines section, looking for any remote access settings or unauthorized guests that might have had previously. Smart home security apps sometimes provide these details in the app, alongside direct notifications for someone using your smart devices without your knowledge.\n\nIf the data doesn't clear it up, consider speaking with a trusted neighbor or friend during the times you're noticing these issues as proof of absence; meanwhile, an increase in security, like installing a network security system with cameras or recording devices, might",
